# Build-context exclusions.
#
# Why this file is not optional: without it `docker build .` uploads
# node_modules (929 MB / ~51,000 files) and .next (the previous build's output)
# into the daemon before a single instruction runs. That is slow, and worse, a
# host-built node_modules contains Windows PE .node addons that would shadow the
# Linux ones installed inside the image - producing a runtime
# "invalid ELF header" that looks like a corrupt image rather than a leaked
# build context.

# Patterns are matched with Go's filepath.Match against the context-relative
# path, so a bare `*.db` would match only the root level and let prisma/dev.db
# through. Every data/dependency pattern below is therefore written with `**/`.

# Dependencies and build output - reinstalled/rebuilt inside the image.
**/node_modules
.next
out
build
dist

# Live data. dev.db is the production database on the dev box; it must be
# bind-mounted at runtime, never baked into an image layer (an image layer is
# immutable and would ship real attendance data to anyone who pulls it).
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
backups
data

# Secrets. Only .env.production.example belongs in the context.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
.env.production

# Local tooling and VCS.
.git
.gitignore
.vscode
.idea
**/*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Tests and docs are not needed by the runtime image. They stay in the repo.
tests
docs
**/*.md

# Sample spreadsheets used for manual import testing (hundreds of KB each).
**/*.xls
**/*.xlsx

# Scratch files. The leading-underscore convention is used for throwaway
# probe scripts during development; none should ever reach a build.
_*
